@NouhJama, we managed to reproduce the issue. It's essentially happening when WSL is used, and the app's source code is stored in the Windows file system (i.e., in Linux, your code is mounted somewhere under the `/mnt` path).

We're working on a fix for this! A temporary workaround would be to move your code anywhere else inside Linux except for the `/mnt` directory (somewhere in `/home/yourusername` would be a good choice).

UPDATE: A fix has been released. Make sure you update your project dependency to get the latest `fastapi-cloud-cli` version

The problem with openapi I mentioned before is:

Imagine your ControllerXX dependencies have required Query parameters

```
class Controller1:
    def __init__(self, limit: int):
        ...

class Controller2:
    def __init__(self, count: int):
        ...
```

In your case there probably no need to add request parameters, but in general dependencies allow this and we will need to handle this.

Now you want to choose one of these controllers depending on payload.
Currently both, `limit` and `count` will be required. But you probably expect only one of them to be required depending on the payload. But it's currently impossible to declare such schema (with conditional Query parameters) in openapi.

Finally I came with this solution:

```python
    if not other_superusers:
        raise DataConflictException(fields(UserUpdate).superuser, "Cannot demote the last superuser")
```

where exception is just derived from HTTPException:

```python
class DataConflictException(HTTPException):
    def __init__(self, field: str, msg: str, error_type: str = "data conflict"):
        self.detail = [{
            "loc": [
                "body",
                field
            ],
            "msg": msg,
            "type": error_type
        }]
        self.status_code = status.HTTP_409_CONFLICT
```

And field name helper is taken from [this discussion](https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic/discussions/8600):

```python
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class _GetFields:
    _model: type[BaseModel]

    def __getattr__(self, item: str) -> Any:
        if item in self._model.model_fields:
            return item

        return getattr(self._model, item)


TModel = TypeVar("TModel", bound=type[BaseModel])


def fields(model: TModel, /) -> TModel:
    return cast(TModel, _GetFields(model))


if not TYPE_CHECKING:
    fields = lru_cache(maxsize=256)(fields)
```

